        'Essential Technology for College Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The essential technology for college is whatever reliably meets your program’s actual requirements. Start with school guidance and course needs before buying devices or subscriptions. A modest, supported setup with secure accounts and dependable backups is more useful than expensive equipment that cannot run required software.</p>

<h2>Check requirements first</h2>
<p>Review the program, department, bookstore, accessibility office, and IT pages. Ask about operating system, processor or memory minimums, specialized applications, testing software, webcams, microphones, ports, campus Wi-Fi, virtual labs, and whether tablets or Chromebooks are acceptable. Requirements can differ within one university.</p>

<h2>Build the core setup</h2>
<p>Most students need reliable access to a computer, charger, current supported browser, school email, learning management system, productivity suite, PDF tools, cloud storage, and multifactor authentication. Headphones with a microphone can improve online meetings. A phone is useful for authentication and alerts but often cannot replace a computer for exams, formatting, coding, or specialized work.</p>

<h2>Plan internet and power</h2>
<p>Know where you can use dependable Wi-Fi and power on campus. Test video calls and large uploads before deadlines. If home internet is inconsistent, identify libraries, labs, loaner hotspots, or other authorized alternatives. Download permitted materials for offline use, but do not assume every quiz, ebook, or licensed application works offline.</p>

<h2>Include accessibility</h2>
<p>Evaluate keyboard, display, captions, text-to-speech, speech input, screen-reader compatibility, color and contrast, hearing-device support, and ergonomic needs. Contact the disability or accessibility office early for effective auxiliary aids or course access. Do not buy specialized equipment solely from a peer recommendation.</p>

<h2>Protect files and accounts</h2>
<p>Use unique passwords through a password manager, enable the strongest MFA the school supports, install updates, encrypt devices, and configure automatic backups. Keep recovery methods current. School cloud storage is convenient but can be limited, administered by the institution, or removed after enrollment ends.</p>

<h2>Control the cost</h2>
<p>Check computer labs, equipment loans, repair programs, virtual desktops, library technology, emergency grants, and institution-provided software before purchasing. Compare warranty, repair time, battery replacement, accessories, and expected supported life—not just the sale price. Save receipts and confirm return rules.</p>

<h2>First-week checklist</h2>
<p>Install only approved software, sign into official portals from bookmarked links, configure notifications, submit a practice file when available, test camera and audio, create a folder system, and restore one test file from backup. Record the IT help desk and after-hours support method somewhere accessible without the school account.</p>

<hr><h2>Neutral sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-technology-accessibility">U.S. Department of Education: technology accessibility</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world">CISA: account and device security basics</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/transforming-teaching-technology-evidence-based-toolkit-higher-education">Institute of Education Sciences: technology in higher education</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Program requirements, accessibility needs, and school IT support should drive technology choices.</em></p>
