        'Choosing a Laptop for School' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Choose a laptop by working backward from required courses and software. Do not buy from a generic “college laptop” list until the department confirms compatibility. Engineering, design, media, data, architecture, and some health programs can have materially different needs from general coursework.</p>

<h2>Get written program requirements</h2>
<p>Confirm supported operating systems, required applications, minimum and recommended hardware, virtualization, graphics needs, testing tools, ports, and whether software runs through a campus virtual lab. Ask how long the recommendation is expected to remain supported. A tablet, Chromebook, or ARM-based computer may not run every desktop or proctoring application.</p>

<h2>Match performance to the workload</h2>
<p>General writing, research, spreadsheets, and video meetings usually need less performance than large datasets, software development, computer-aided design, geographic information systems, simulations, or video production. Use the program’s recommended—not merely minimum—specification when the laptop must last several years.</p>

<h2>Evaluate daily usability</h2>
<p>Compare weight, battery life under realistic use, screen size, keyboard, webcam, microphone, charger size, Wi-Fi, and needed ports. Consider an external display or keyboard for long sessions. Try accessibility features and input methods before the return period ends.</p>

<h2>Plan repair and support</h2>
<p>Check warranty length, accidental-damage coverage, local service, turnaround time, replaceable storage or battery, and whether opening the device affects coverage. Ask whether the school provides loaners during repair. Backups are still necessary; a warranty replaces hardware, not lost assignments.</p>

<h2>Compare the total cost</h2>
<p>Include required software, adapters, case, external storage, warranty, tax, and likely repairs. Check campus purchasing agreements, refurbished-business devices, loan programs, labs, and financial-aid cost-of-attendance procedures. Avoid financing based only on a monthly payment.</p>

<h2>Secure and maintain it</h2>
<p>Use a supported operating system, automatic security updates, device encryption, screen lock, MFA, and an automatic backup. Install software from the institution or publisher rather than download sites. Label the device without exposing a student ID and record its serial number privately.</p>

<h2>Decision rule</h2>
<p>Buy only when the device meets program compatibility, accessibility, repair, battery, and budget requirements with reasonable margin. If requirements are unclear, wait or use campus equipment temporarily. Return incompatible hardware within the seller’s deadline rather than hoping a required tool will work later.</p>

<hr><h2>Neutral sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/computers">ENERGY STAR: computer types and buying guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-technology-accessibility">Department of Education: accessible technology</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world">CISA: updates, passwords, and MFA</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Verify the current program specification and return policy before purchasing.</em></p>
