        'Microsoft 365 for Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Microsoft 365 Education can provide Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OneNote, Outlook, Teams, OneDrive, and other services through a school account. The exact apps, desktop installations, storage, AI features, and length of access depend on the institution’s license and administrator settings.</p>

<h2>Confirm what your school provides</h2>
<p>Sign in through the institution’s official portal and review the assigned license. Some plans provide web apps only; others include desktop applications or additional services. Do not purchase a personal subscription until checking school access, required app versions, and whether a course needs a Windows-only feature.</p>

<h2>Separate school and personal identities</h2>
<p>A school Microsoft account is managed by the institution and is different from a personal Microsoft account even when names look similar. Watch which account is active before saving, sharing, joining Teams, or using AI features. Follow institutional rules for school records and sensitive data.</p>

<h2>Organize files deliberately</h2>
<p>Create clear term and course folders in OneDrive and use meaningful file names. Confirm that the sync indicator shows successful upload before closing a device. Sync improves availability across devices, but accidental deletion or account loss can still affect synced files; keep an independent backup of important permitted work.</p>

<h2>Collaborate without losing ownership</h2>
<p>Use share links with the narrowest appropriate permission and verify recipients. Learn comments, tracked changes, version history, and file ownership. For group work, agree on the authoritative copy and submission owner. Never assume a Teams message or shared file counts as an LMS submission.</p>

<h2>Check formatting and offline access</h2>
<p>Web and desktop apps can differ, and conversion to PDF or another office format can change fonts, formulas, citations, or layout. Open the final file after export. If internet access is uncertain, configure permitted offline files and test them before travel or an outage.</p>

<h2>Secure the account</h2>
<p>Use the school’s MFA, never approve an unexpected sign-in prompt, and report suspicious messages through the designated method. Keep recovery information current where the institution permits it. IT—not a classmate—should resolve license or account-lock problems.</p>

<h2>Plan for graduation or departure</h2>
<p>School eligibility and online services can end after enrollment or employment ends. Before the published deadline, export eligible personal work, transfer ownership where allowed, preserve nonconfidential contacts, and remove school files from shared or personal devices according to policy. Do not copy restricted research, records, or licensed content.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/education/products/office">Microsoft: Office 365 Education eligibility and access</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onedrive">Microsoft Support: OneDrive</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/microsoft-365-accessibility-center-resources-for-people-with-disabilities-ecab0fcf-d143-4fe8-a2ff-6cd596bddc6d">Microsoft 365 Accessibility Center</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Your institution’s license, acceptable-use policy, and account-retention schedule control.</em></p>
