        'Google Workspace for Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Google Workspace for Education commonly includes Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms, Meet, Classroom, and related services. The school creates and administers the account, chooses which tools are enabled, and can set storage, sharing, age, and third-party-app restrictions.</p>

<h2>Know which account is active</h2>
<p>A school Workspace account is managed separately from a personal Google account. Use distinct browser profiles or clear account labels to avoid saving assignments to the wrong Drive, sending from the wrong address, or granting a personal add-on access to school data.</p>

<h2>Organize Drive and ownership</h2>
<p>Create term and course folders and use consistent file names. “Shared with me” does not necessarily mean you own or control the file. For group work, determine who owns the authoritative copy, who can edit, and what happens when a member leaves. Use limited sharing rather than public links unless the assignment requires publication.</p>

<h2>Use collaboration tools carefully</h2>
<p>Comments, suggestions, version history, and named edits can support group work and show process. Resolve rather than erase useful feedback only when appropriate. A link in Docs or Classroom is not automatically a final LMS submission; confirm the required destination and submission receipt.</p>

<h2>Plan for formats and offline work</h2>
<p>Exports to Microsoft Office, PDF, images, or other formats can alter layout, formulas, fonts, speaker notes, or accessibility. Inspect the exported file. Enable offline access only on a trusted device and test it in advance; not every institutional tool or linked resource works without internet.</p>

<h2>Understand storage and administration</h2>
<p>Education storage is pooled at the organization level, and administrators can impose individual limits. Features and third-party apps may be disabled. Treat the account as institutional: follow privacy, records, research, and acceptable-use rules and contact the administrator when access is blocked.</p>

<h2>Secure the account</h2>
<p>Use unique credentials, school-required multifactor authentication, updated recovery methods, and prompt software updates. Check share settings before sending links and do not approve unexpected sign-ins. Report suspected phishing or account compromise immediately through official IT channels.</p>

<h2>Prepare to leave</h2>
<p>Schools decide when graduate or former-student access ends. Before that date, transfer or download eligible personal work using permitted methods and move ownership of group or organization files appropriately. Do not export education records, confidential research, licensed materials, or files you do not have permission to retain.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://edu.google.com/intl/ALL_us/workspace-for-education/editions/education-fundamentals/">Google Workspace for Education Fundamentals</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://support.google.com/accounts/answer/6250166">Google: work or school accounts are administrator-managed</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/10403871">Google: Workspace for Education pooled storage</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Your school’s edition, administrator settings, privacy rules, and retention schedule control.</em></p>
