        'How Learning Management Systems Work' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A learning management system (LMS) is the school’s online environment for course materials, announcements, discussions, assignments, quizzes, grades, and links to other tools. Common systems include Canvas, Blackboard Learn, Brightspace, and Moodle, but every institution and instructor can configure them differently.</p>

<h2>Start with the course’s official instructions</h2>
<p>Read the syllabus, welcome announcement, module sequence, and instructor directions. The LMS displays configured information; it does not decide the course policy. If the syllabus, assignment page, calendar, or verbal instruction conflicts, ask the instructor in writing before the deadline.</p>

<h2>Understand dates</h2>
<p>A due date, availability start, availability end, close date, and late deadline can be different. A calendar may omit activities that were not configured with dates. Copy every requirement to your own calendar and include the time zone. Do not assume an item remaining visible means it can still be submitted for credit.</p>

<h2>Submit deliberately</h2>
<p>Open the correct course and assignment, verify file type and name, upload the final version, complete every confirmation step, and save the receipt or submission history. Reopen the submission if possible. A file stored in cloud drive, attached to a message, or shown as uploaded is not necessarily submitted.</p>

<h2>Use the gradebook as a report</h2>
<p>Grades can be hidden, unposted, manually entered, exempted, weighted, or missing. A running percentage may exclude ungraded work and may not equal the final course grade. Read feedback and compare categories with the syllabus. Ask the instructor about discrepancies rather than editing your own forecast into an official result.</p>

<h2>Configure notifications</h2>
<p>Choose useful email or app alerts for announcements, changed dates, feedback, and grades, but check the LMS directly on a routine schedule. Notifications can be delayed, disabled, filtered, or dependent on instructor settings. They are a convenience, not the sole deadline system.</p>

<h2>Handle integrations carefully</h2>
<p>Publisher homework, video, proctoring, plagiarism review, and cloud tools may open inside or outside the LMS. Confirm that work syncs back to the correct course and account. Review privacy notices and use only tools required or approved by the institution.</p>

<h2>Document technical problems</h2>
<p>Capture the time, course, assignment, error text, browser, device, and troubleshooting attempted without exposing passwords or private records. Contact the help desk and instructor promptly. A help ticket documents the issue but does not itself extend a deadline; the course’s policy controls.</p>

<hr><h2>Official platform guides</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://community.canvaslms.com/t5/Canvas-Student-Guide/tkb-p/student">Canvas Student Guide</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://help.blackboard.com/Learn/Student">Blackboard Learn Student Help</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://community.d2l.com/brightspace/kb/categories/160-learner">Brightspace learner resources</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://docs.moodle.org/en/Student_FAQ">Moodle Student FAQ</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. The syllabus, assignment instructions, instructor, and institution’s LMS configuration control.</em></p>
