        'Canvas, Blackboard, Brightspace & Moodle Basics' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Canvas, Blackboard Learn, Brightspace, and Moodle organize the same broad course activities in different ways. Schools can rename navigation items, enable integrations, change the interface, and run different versions, so use the institution’s own help pages alongside vendor documentation.</p>

<h2>Canvas</h2>
<p>Canvas commonly organizes work through Modules, Assignments, Discussions, Quizzes, Calendar, To Do, Inbox, and Grades. A module can require items in sequence. The To Do list is helpful but may not represent every syllabus obligation. Confirm a submitted status and open submission details after uploading.</p>

<h2>Blackboard Learn</h2>
<p>Blackboard courses may use Ultra Course View or an institution’s supported configuration. Content, Activity Stream, Calendar, Messages, Discussions, Assignments, Tests, and Gradebook locations can vary. Check whether an item saves a draft or completes a submission and whether feedback requires opening the assessment.</p>

<h2>Brightspace</h2>
<p>Brightspace often uses Content, Assignments, Quizzes, Discussions, Grades, Calendar, and notifications, with menus chosen by the institution. Due and end dates serve different purposes. Use submission history for uploaded work and remember that grades or feedback appear only when the instructor publishes them.</p>

<h2>Moodle</h2>
<p>Moodle is open-source and highly configurable. Courses may use sections, activities, resources, assignments, quizzes, forums, completion tracking, calendar, and gradebook. Names and layouts can differ substantially by theme and version. Confirm whether an assignment requires a final submit step after saving a draft.</p>

<h2>Practices that work across platforms</h2>
<p>Read the syllabus and announcements, open every course area during the first week, configure notifications, copy dates to a separate calendar, use supported browsers, and test file uploads. Save receipts and keep local or independently backed-up copies of submitted work.</p>

<h2>Mobile apps and accessibility</h2>
<p>Apps are useful for alerts and reading, but some quizzes, integrations, formatting, and submission flows work differently from a full browser. Use the school-supported method for high-stakes tasks. Contact accessibility services when course technology creates a barrier; do not wait until an exam begins.</p>

<h2>When something looks wrong</h2>
<p>Refresh, confirm the correct course and account, check dates and file requirements, try the supported browser, and consult the school status page. Document the exact error and contact both IT and the instructor promptly. Never share credentials with someone offering to “fix” the course.</p>

<hr><h2>Official platform guides</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://community.canvaslms.com/t5/Canvas-Student-Guide/tkb-p/student">Canvas Student Guide</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://help.blackboard.com/Learn/Student">Blackboard Learn Student Help</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://community.d2l.com/brightspace/kb/categories/160-learner">Brightspace learner resources</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://docs.moodle.org/en/Student_FAQ">Moodle Student FAQ</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Product versions and school configurations vary; use local support instructions.</em></p>
