        'Keeping Your Student Accounts Secure' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Your school email often unlocks the LMS, cloud files, registration, financial aid, payroll, library access, and password resets. Protect it as a primary identity account. A compromised account can expose both personal information and other students’ data.</p>

<h2>Use unique credentials</h2>
<p>Never reuse the school password on shopping, social, gaming, or job sites. Use a reputable password manager to generate and store long, unique passwords. Do not share a password with classmates, family, tutors, supervisors, or anyone claiming to be IT.</p>

<h2>Turn on strong MFA</h2>
<p>Enroll every method the school recommends and prefer phishing-resistant options such as security keys or passkeys when supported. Save recovery codes securely and register a backup method. Never approve an unexpected push, provide a one-time code to another person, or accept repeated prompts just to stop them.</p>

<h2>Secure devices and sessions</h2>
<p>Install operating-system, browser, and application updates promptly. Use screen lock, device encryption, and supported security software. Do not leave accounts signed in on shared computers; use the official sign-out process and avoid saving passwords. Treat public charging cables and unknown USB devices cautiously.</p>

<h2>Control sharing and connected apps</h2>
<p>Review cloud links, shared folders, mailbox forwarding, delegated access, app permissions, and active sessions. Remove access that is no longer needed. A third-party study tool connected to the school account may gain access to files or profile data; use only school-approved integrations.</p>

<h2>Keep recovery information current</h2>
<p>Update permitted recovery phone numbers and email addresses before changing devices or graduating. Store the official help-desk contact separately. Security questions should not use facts easily found online. Never pay someone on social media to recover an institutional account.</p>

<h2>Recognize suspicious activity</h2>
<p>Warning signs include password-reset notices you did not request, sent mail you do not recognize, new forwarding rules, unfamiliar MFA methods, missing files, or altered aid and payroll information. Do not investigate by clicking the alert’s link; open the known official portal independently.</p>

<h2>Respond quickly</h2>
<p>From a trusted device, contact IT, change the password, revoke sessions or connected apps if permitted, and review recovery methods, forwarding, files, financial details, and submitted work. Preserve suspicious messages for reporting. If personal financial or identity information was exposed, follow the school’s incident guidance and the appropriate government recovery steps.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world">CISA Secure Our World</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/more-password">CISA: multifactor authentication</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.identitytheft.gov/">Federal Trade Commission: IdentityTheft.gov</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Follow your institution’s security and incident-reporting procedures.</em></p>
