        'Avoiding Phishing Emails Targeting Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Student phishing messages imitate trusted campus services and exploit urgency. Common lures involve account closure, financial-aid refunds, payroll, jobs, shared documents, invoices, library access, housing, parking, package delivery, or an instructor asking for an unusual favor.</p>

<h2>Pause at urgency or secrecy</h2>
<p>Be suspicious when a message demands immediate action, threatens account loss, promises easy money, discourages verification, or requests credentials, MFA codes, gift cards, cryptocurrency, check deposits, or money transfers. A familiar logo, display name, or copied signature does not prove the sender.</p>

<h2>Inspect the real destination</h2>
<p>Check the complete sender address and domain. On a computer, preview a link before opening it; on mobile, use the platform’s link-preview method without navigating. Look for misspellings, unrelated domains, shortened links, and fake sign-in pages. Do not open unexpected attachments, QR codes, or shared documents.</p>

<h2>Verify through a known channel</h2>
<p>Open the school portal from a bookmark or type the known address instead of using the message link. Call the office using the number on its official website. Contact the supposed sender through a new message or established number. Do not use contact information supplied inside the suspicious message.</p>

<h2>Recognize job and payment scams</h2>
<p>Scammers may impersonate professors or employers, conduct text-only interviews, send a check for “equipment,” and demand that part of the money be returned. A deposited check can appear available before it is discovered to be fraudulent. Legitimate employers do not require applicants to pay for a job or forward money.</p>

<h2>Use MFA safely</h2>
<p>MFA reduces risk but attackers may ask for the code or trigger repeated approval prompts. Approve only a sign-in you initiated. If an unexpected prompt appears, deny it, change the password through the official portal, and contact IT. Phishing-resistant MFA is preferable when offered.</p>

<h2>Report rather than only delete</h2>
<p>Use the institution’s phishing-report button or security address so IT can protect others. Preserve the message until reporting is complete, then follow instructions. Report financial scams to the appropriate bank, payment service, law enforcement, or Federal Trade Commission as applicable.</p>

<h2>If you clicked or responded</h2>
<p>Disconnect from the suspicious site, contact IT immediately, and use a trusted device to change affected passwords and revoke sessions. Review MFA methods, mailbox forwarding, cloud sharing, financial accounts, payroll, and aid information. Do not hide the mistake; faster reporting can limit harm.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing">CISA: recognize and report phishing</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2025/05/college-students-avoid-scammers-while-you-job-hunt">FTC: job scams targeting college students</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">Federal Trade Commission: ReportFraud</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Verify messages through independently located campus contact information.</em></p>
