        'Backing Up Your Schoolwork' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A backup is a recoverable additional copy of your work. Saving repeatedly to one laptop or synchronizing one cloud folder is not enough: deletion, corruption, ransomware, account loss, theft, and sync mistakes can affect the only copy or spread to connected copies.</p>

<h2>Identify irreplaceable work</h2>
<p>Prioritize drafts, notes, data, code, research records, portfolios, presentations, creative files, and capstone work. Include configuration files, citations, and documentation needed to reproduce the result. Follow research, privacy, clinical, and employer rules; some data cannot be placed in personal cloud storage.</p>

<h2>Use multiple copies</h2>
<p>A practical model is the 3-2-1 approach: maintain the working copy plus two backups, use more than one type of storage, and keep one copy in a different location. For example, combine a laptop, institution-approved cloud service with version history, and a disconnected encrypted drive.</p>

<h2>Distinguish sync, versioning, and backup</h2>
<p>Sync keeps files available across devices but can copy accidental deletion or corruption. Version history may restore earlier states but has service-specific limits. A separate backup reduces dependence on one account or provider. Learn what each tool actually retains and for how long.</p>

<h2>Automate a routine</h2>
<p>Enable permitted automatic backup and cloud autosave, then schedule a second backup for important work. Before major edits, submission, travel, device repair, or software upgrades, create a named checkpoint. Use clear filenames and dates rather than repeatedly overwriting an ambiguous “final” file.</p>

<h2>Protect sensitive backups</h2>
<p>Encrypt devices and removable media, use MFA on cloud accounts, limit sharing, and store recovery keys safely. Disconnect external drives when not backing up so ransomware or accidents cannot reach them. Never place protected student, patient, research-participant, or employer data in an unapproved service.</p>

<h2>Test restoration</h2>
<p>Periodically restore a sample folder to a different location and open the files. Confirm that file formats, linked assets, code dependencies, and version history work. A green sync icon or completed progress bar is not proof that a usable recovery exists.</p>

<h2>Plan for account closure</h2>
<p>Before graduation, transfer, or leave, learn when school storage ends. Export eligible personal work early and preserve readable formats. Move shared-file ownership when allowed. Do not copy licensed course materials, confidential data, or group work you lack permission to retain.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/training/how-protect-data-stored-your-devices">CISA: protecting and backing up device data</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/publications/data_backup_options.pdf">CISA: data backup options and the 3-2-1 model</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/1800/25/final">NIST: protecting data integrity and backups</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Follow institutional data rules and test that backups can actually be restored.</em></p>
HTML,

        'Online Class Survival Guide' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Online courses trade travel time for greater responsibility in finding instructions, planning work, and solving technology problems. They can be synchronous, asynchronous, hybrid, self-paced, or deadline-driven. Read the delivery details rather than assuming “online” means flexible at every hour.</p>

<h2>Complete the orientation</h2>
<p>Before classes begin, sign into the LMS, open every course area, read the syllabus, configure notifications, and test required software. Locate live meeting links, office hours, quizzes, discussions, assignments, grades, and support. Complete practice submissions or technology checks when offered.</p>

<h2>Build one deadline system</h2>
<p>Copy due dates, live meetings, availability windows, exams, and group commitments into a calendar with the correct time zone. Schedule the work needed before each deadline. Check announcements and the LMS directly several times a week because notifications can fail or be delayed.</p>

<h2>Create a weekly routine</h2>
<p>Reserve consistent blocks for reading, videos, participation, assignments, and review. Break large tasks into visible steps. Avoid treating asynchronous work as optional until the due date. Use a location and browser profile that reduce distractions and separate school from personal accounts.</p>

<h2>Participate with purpose</h2>
<p>Follow discussion and meeting expectations, contribute original work, and respond substantively. For group projects, agree on roles, file ownership, internal deadlines, and communication. Protect classmates’ privacy; do not record, repost, or feed course conversations into other tools without permission.</p>

<h2>Prepare for technology failure</h2>
<p>Use a supported browser, install updates before high-stakes work, keep local or permitted offline copies, and maintain backups. Identify alternate internet and device access. If a problem occurs, capture the exact error and time, contact IT and the instructor promptly, and follow the course’s late-work policy.</p>

<h2>Use help early</h2>
<p>Attend virtual office hours, ask focused questions, and use tutoring, library, advising, and technical support. Online students remain eligible for campus services according to institutional rules. Silence can look like disengagement, so communicate before a small problem becomes a missed course.</p>

<h2>Address accessibility and privacy</h2>
<p>Request accommodations through the responsible office and report inaccessible videos, documents, testing tools, or interfaces early. Use school-approved services for protected data. Understand camera, recording, proctoring, and identity-verification requirements before enrolling when possible, and ask about effective alternatives through official processes.</p>

<hr><h2>Neutral sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://ies.ed.gov/learn/blog/transforming-teaching-technology-evidence-based-toolkit-higher-education">Institute of Education Sciences: technology-supported postsecondary learning</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/laws-and-policy/civil-rights-laws/disability-discrimination/disability-discrimination-key-issues/disability-discrimination-technology-accessibility">Department of Education: digital accessibility</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world">CISA: secure devices and accounts</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. The syllabus, course format, accessibility plan, and institution’s support procedures control.</em></p>
