        'Free & Discounted Software for Students' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Students may receive software through an institution, a publisher’s education plan, or a temporary student discount. “Free for students” is not universal: eligibility, features, storage, commercial-use rights, renewal, and post-graduation access can differ.</p>

<h2>Start with the school catalog</h2>
<p>Check the IT software portal, library, department, virtual computer lab, and bookstore before buying. Schools may provide Microsoft 365, statistical tools, creative applications, citation managers, security software, cloud services, developer tools, or remote access to specialized lab software.</p>

<h2>Confirm the license type</h2>
<p>Ask whether access is web-only or includes desktop installation, which devices and operating systems are supported, how many activations are allowed, and whether use is limited to coursework. Educational licenses may prohibit commercial work or require periodic enrollment verification.</p>

<h2>Check eligibility and renewal</h2>
<p>Read the current publisher terms rather than an old discount list. A school email may start verification without guaranteeing approval. Note the regular price, renewal date, automatic billing, cancellation process, and what happens when student status ends. A first-year promotional price may increase later.</p>

<h2>Protect data and accounts</h2>
<p>Use the official school portal or publisher site, not a marketplace key or cracked download. Pirated software can contain malware and lacks reliable updates. Before connecting a third-party app to the school account, review requested permissions and school policy. Do not place protected data in an unapproved service.</p>

<h2>Plan file portability</h2>
<p>Know which file formats remain readable without the subscription. Export important work to appropriate standard formats while preserving editable originals when permitted. Transfer eligible files before graduation and remove institution-licensed software if the agreement requires it.</p>

<h2>Compare total cost and alternatives</h2>
<p>A free tool may require paid storage, proprietary hardware, or a later subscription. Compare open-source software, campus labs, browser versions, short-term licenses, and course-specific access codes. Verify that an alternative preserves required formatting and compatibility before an assignment is due.</p>

<h2>Examples, not guarantees</h2>
<p>Microsoft offers qualifying education access; GitHub maintains a verified student developer program; Autodesk offers qualifying educational access. Other vendors change offers frequently. Confirm current terms directly and with campus IT, especially for AI features, cloud quotas, or professional certification.</p>

<hr><h2>Official program pages</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/education/products/office">Microsoft Office 365 Education</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://education.github.com/pack">GitHub Student Developer Pack</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.autodesk.com/education/edu-software/overview">Autodesk Education access</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Offers change; verify current eligibility, renewal, use rights, and data-retention terms.</em></p>
