        'Using AI Tools Responsibly' => <<<'HTML'
<p>AI tools can help brainstorm, explain concepts, organize questions, practice, translate, code, analyze, or create media—but they can also produce confident errors, invented citations, biased output, privacy exposure, and work that violates a course rule. Responsible use begins with permission and ends with human verification.</p>

<h2>Check permission before use</h2>
<p>Read the assignment, syllabus, course announcements, program policy, and tool instructions. Policies may allow one use and prohibit another, such as permitting grammar feedback but not generated prose. When unclear, describe the exact intended use to the instructor and ask before submitting.</p>

<h2>Protect private and restricted information</h2>
<p>Do not paste student records, unpublished research, patient or client information, employer data, exams, recommendation letters, private discussions, personal identifiers, or copyrighted course materials into an unapproved AI service. A public consumer tool is not automatically authorized for institutional data.</p>

<h2>Verify every important output</h2>
<p>Generative systems can fabricate facts, sources, quotations, calculations, code behavior, and legal or medical claims. Locate and read the original sources, reproduce calculations, run tests, and compare against authoritative material. Never cite a source you have not verified exists and supports the statement.</p>

<h2>Keep human judgment and authorship</h2>
<p>Use the tool to support learning rather than replace the thinking the assignment measures. Decide the argument, evaluate evidence, write or revise meaningfully, and remain able to explain every submitted element. Do not present generated experience, data, lab work, observations, or citations as real.</p>

<h2>Disclose and document use</h2>
<p>Follow the required citation or acknowledgment format. When no format is specified, keep a record of tool name and version when available, date, prompts, outputs used, and how you verified or changed them. Disclosure does not make a prohibited use acceptable, but it supports transparency when use is allowed.</p>

<h2>Evaluate fairness, access, and ownership</h2>
<p>Check whether output stereotypes people, omits perspectives, creates accessibility barriers, or imitates a living creator inappropriately. Understand that copyright in AI-assisted or generated work is fact-specific and depends on human authorship and other law. Do not assume a service grants exclusive rights to its output.</p>

<h2>Use a simple decision test</h2>
<p>Ask: Is this use permitted? Can the data be shared? Does the tool support the learning goal? Can I verify the result? Can I explain and disclose the process? Would I be comfortable showing the instructor the prompts and revisions? If any answer is no, pause and use another method.</p>

<hr><h2>Neutral sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework">NIST AI Risk Management Framework and Generative AI Profile</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.ed.gov/about/news/press-release/us-department-of-education-issues-guidance-artificial-intelligence-use-schools-proposes-additional-supplemental-priority">Department of Education: responsible AI guidance</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology">Department of Education: privacy and education technology</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Course permission, institutional privacy rules, and assignment requirements control.</em></p>
