        'AI and Academic Integrity' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Academic integrity means submitting work that truthfully represents your own learning and permitted assistance. AI use is not automatically allowed or automatically misconduct; the answer depends on the assignment, instructor, course, program, institution, and professional standards.</p>

<h2>Find the controlling rule</h2>
<p>Read the exact assignment, syllabus, academic-integrity code, department rules, and instructor announcements. A general university AI statement may not override a more specific assignment direction. Policies can change between courses and terms, so prior permission is not transferable.</p>

<h2>Distinguish types of use</h2>
<p>A course may treat brainstorming, tutoring, grammar help, translation, coding suggestions, data analysis, image generation, outlining, rewriting, and answer generation differently. The relevant question is not merely “Did you use AI?” but what work the tool performed and whether that use was permitted and disclosed.</p>

<h2>Avoid misrepresentation</h2>
<p>Do not submit generated text, code, analysis, citations, images, calculations, data, interviews, laboratory results, or reflections as your own when the rules prohibit it. Do not use AI to fabricate process evidence or hide another unauthorized source. Being able to edit output does not necessarily make its use authorized.</p>

<h2>Verify sources and facts</h2>
<p>AI systems can invent citations and confidently state false information. Read every cited source and confirm it supports the claim. Check calculations, code, quotations, dates, and primary evidence. Fabricated sources can be academic misconduct even when the student did not intend to invent them.</p>

<h2>Disclose allowed assistance</h2>
<p>Use the instructor’s requested acknowledgment or citation method and retain prompts, relevant outputs, drafts, revision history, notes, and verification steps. State the tool, purpose, and contribution accurately. Disclosure cannot cure prohibited use, but failing to disclose required assistance can create a separate integrity problem.</p>

<h2>Protect the assessment</h2>
<p>Do not upload exam questions, assignment materials, classmates’ work, feedback, answer keys, or restricted course content to an unapproved service. Do not use a tool during a closed-resource assessment unless expressly permitted. Follow research, privacy, copyright, clinical, and professional rules in addition to academic policy.</p>

<h2>If questioned</h2>
<p>Preserve drafts and records, read the allegation and procedure, respond truthfully, and distinguish tool use from the work you personally completed. Use the institution’s advisor, student support, or appeal process where available. Do not delete evidence, invent a history, or ask an AI tool to create false drafts.</p>

<h2>Before submitting</h2>
<p>Confirm permission, authorship, factual accuracy, citations, privacy, and disclosure. Be able to explain the reasoning and reproduce essential work without relying on the generated output. If unsure, ask the instructor before the deadline and keep the written answer.</p>

<hr><h2>Neutral sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework">NIST AI Risk Management Framework</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.copyright.gov/ai/">U.S. Copyright Office: copyright and artificial intelligence</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology">Department of Education: privacy and education technology</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. The specific assignment, instructor, course, program, and academic-integrity process control.</em></p>
